Hello, 

I reviewed the document and here is my feedback:

----

I agree that for Significant Events such as:

* {{Driver Carrying Weapons}}
* {{Driver Transporting Illegal Drugs}}
* {{Driver Threatens Terrorist Activity}}
* {{Driver Robbed or Attempted to Rob Rider}}
* {{Driver Arrested by Authorities}}

We ask for _administrative offense documentation,_ but we need to know exactly what it is, how it is called in Lithuanian, and where drivers can get it. 

These cases are already flagged to the local team in  _#lithuania-high-risk-escalations_. Agents can escalate to OPS once the documentation has been received.

----

What type of administrative offense documentation would be needed in case of accidents? 

* {{Driver Strikes Pedestrian Whilst On Journey}}
* {{Non-Accident Related Injury to Rider During Order}}
* {{Driver Strikes Cyclist Whilst On Journey}}
* {{Driver in Vehicle Accident Causing Injury or Structural Damage}}
* {{Driver in Vehicle Accident Resulting in any Cosmetic Damage}}

----

For Low-risk Event Categories ({{Driver Speeding}}), asking for administrative offense documentation would create too much back and forth for CS Safety, especially because in most cases such reports cannot be confirmed and they are high volumes. 

----

What type of administrative offense documentation would be needed in case of {{Driver Intoxicated Whilst Operating Vehicle}}? If this is a confirmed event or a second report, then the tool already suggests a hard block.

----

The following Event Categories are High-Risk and we’ll follow the high-risk event investigation procedure:

* {{Not Fit for Purpose Vehicle Use}}
* {{Driver Arrived In Incorrect Vehicle}}
* {{Driver Was Not The Same as App}}

----

I have a few other points:

* Do we have any specific regulatory requirements that require us to ask for such documentation? 
* Is your team willing to take ownership of the process/part of the process? For instance, you can get all cases with the {{outcome: hard-block}} escalated and confirm whether you want to apply it or take any other action. 
** The International CS Safety team follows global guidelines. Of course, some local needs can be taken into consideration, but too many exceptions will just open more room for mistakes. 

Open to having a chat in the next weeks if needed. 

----

Cheers, 

Alessandro

We do have unsubscribe button in all of our email so I think this is already covered.




